11:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L23",null,{"guide":{"date":"February–July 1862, with a preview of the western campaigns of late 1862","place":"The western theater from Missouri and Arkansas to Kentucky, Tennessee, Mississippi, Alabama, and the Appalachian gateway at Cumberland Gap","result":"By early summer 1862, United States forces had broken the Confederacy’s first western defensive line, occupied Nashville, seized the railroad junction at Corinth, won control of Memphis, reached Helena on the Mississippi, occupied northern Alabama, and taken Cumberland Gap. Those gains did not produce an immediate drive into the Deep South. Henry W. Halleck dispersed forces to secure a long river-and-rail supply network, while distance, weak transportation links, and Confederate positions at Vicksburg, eastern Tennessee, and Chattanooga limited the next advances. The pause gave Confederate commanders time to prepare counteroffensives in Kentucky, Tennessee, and Mississippi","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"A strategic reset in the western theater after Shiloh and Corinth","note":"The episode surveys the position reached by mid-1862 and introduces the geographic and logistical constraints behind the Kentucky, Corinth, and Stones River campaigns covered next."},{"label":"Upper South at stake","value":"Kentucky and Tennessee linked population, food production, rivers, roads, and railroads","note":"Holding the region protected the Confederate interior; losing it placed United States armies near Mississippi, Alabama, Georgia, and the principal east-west railroad through Chattanooga."},{"label":"Three campaigns previewed","value":"Bragg in Kentucky, Van Dorn at Corinth, and Rosecrans at Stones River","note":"The episode previews Confederate attempts to recover the initiative and the United States response. It does not narrate those campaigns in detail."},{"label":"Opening victories","value":"Forts Henry and Donelson fell in February 1862","note":"The collapse of the Tennessee and Cumberland River positions forced the Confederacy to abandon Kentucky and much of Middle and West Tennessee."},{"label":"Nashville occupied","value":"First Confederate state capital captured by United States forces","note":"Advance elements of the Army of the Ohio reached Nashville on February 23, and Don Carlos Buell accepted the city’s formal surrender on February 25. Nashville remained an essential United States supply base."},{"label":"Shiloh","value":"April 6–7, 1862; United States victory after a Confederate surprise attack","note":"Albert Sidney Johnston was mortally wounded on April 6. P. G. T. Beauregard assumed command, and the reinforced United States armies drove his force back toward Corinth on April 7."},{"label":"Why Corinth mattered","value":"The Memphis & Charleston and Mobile & Ohio railroads crossed there","note":"The east-west line linked the Mississippi Valley with Chattanooga and the Atlantic states; the north-south line connected western Tennessee with central Mississippi and Mobile."},{"label":"United States concentration","value":"More than 100,000 soldiers in three armies","note":"Halleck personally assembled Grant’s Army of the Tennessee, Buell’s Army of the Ohio, and Pope’s Army of the Mississippi for the advance on Corinth. National Park Service interpretation gives the combined strength as about 120,000."},{"label":"Siege of Corinth","value":"April 29–May 30, 1862","note":"Halleck advanced slowly, entrenched after successive movements, and approached the Confederate defenses without risking the kind of surprise suffered at Shiloh."},{"label":"Confederate evacuation","value":"Beauregard withdrew to Tupelo during the night of May 29–30","note":"Outnumbered and suffering from disease and poor water, the Confederates removed troops and supplies by rail while deceptive cheering, train noise, campfires, and dummy guns concealed the movement."},{"label":"Strategic gain, incomplete destruction","value":"The United States took Corinth; Beauregard preserved his army","note":"Halleck secured the railroad crossing and northern Mississippi position, but the Confederate field army escaped roughly sixty miles south to Tupelo."},{"label":"Fort Pillow and Memphis","value":"Fort Pillow was evacuated June 4; Memphis fell June 6","note":"At Memphis, Flag Officer Charles H. Davis’s gunboats and Col. Charles Ellet’s rams destroyed or captured all but one vessel of the Confederate River Defense Fleet. United States forces then occupied the city."},{"label":"Mississippi River remained contested","value":"Memphis opened another long reach of river, but Vicksburg still blocked complete control","note":"The episode also names Port Hudson. Confederate forces did not begin fortifying Port Hudson until later in 1862, so it should not be treated as an established strongpoint at the exact moment Memphis fell."},{"label":"Pea Ridge","value":"March 7–8, 1862; United States victory in northwest Arkansas","note":"Samuel R. Curtis defeated Earl Van Dorn’s larger Confederate army. The victory secured Missouri for the United States and ended the immediate Confederate effort to recover that state."},{"label":"New Mexico campaign","value":"The Confederate invasion failed after Glorieta Pass, March 26–28","note":"United States troops destroyed the Confederate supply train at Johnson’s Ranch. Without those supplies, Henry H. Sibley’s force abandoned its advance toward Fort Union and ultimately retreated to Texas."},{"label":"Why Halleck paused","value":"The newly occupied network had to be supplied and defended","note":"Halleck dispersed troops among key towns, railroads, rivers, and depots rather than moving his entire concentration south after Beauregard."},{"label":"Corinth’s railroad distinction","value":"A junction of two lines, but only one principal rail corridor continued south","note":"The Mobile & Ohio Railroad offered the main rail penetration from Corinth toward Tupelo and central Mississippi. Moving a very large army along that single axis created a different problem from occupying the well-connected Upper South."},{"label":"Upper South logistics","value":"Shorter distances and a denser transportation network","note":"The Tennessee, Cumberland, and Mississippi Rivers, together with established roads and railroads, allowed United States forces to move inland and receive supplies from northern bases."},{"label":"Deep South logistics","value":"Longer distances, fewer towns, thinner rail coverage, and limited navigable interior rivers","note":"The episode contrasts the Deep South’s landlocked interior with the Upper South. Large armies could not occupy every locality; campaigns had to follow selected rivers, railroads, and strategically valuable corridors."},{"label":"Narrow axes of advance","value":"The Mississippi River and the Chattanooga–Atlanta railroad corridor","note":"The map logic of the western war concentrated future operations along transportation lines capable of sustaining armies over great distances."},{"label":"Curtis’s supply problem","value":"A wagon route of more than 200 miles from Rolla, Missouri","note":"At Batesville, Curtis’s Army of the Southwest could not sustain an advance on Little Rock through an increasingly disrupted overland supply line."},{"label":"Curtis reaches Helena","value":"July 12, 1862","note":"Curtis cut loose from his Rolla supply line, lived off the countryside, and marched to the Mississippi. Helena restored river supply and became an advanced United States base south of Memphis."},{"label":"Civilian cost of Curtis’s march","value":"Foraging and destruction accompanied the movement to Helena","note":"National Park Service accounts describe the army taking food and destroying property deemed economically or militarily useful to the Confederacy. Thousands of enslaved people also reached the army and moved toward freedom."},{"label":"Mitchel in northern Alabama","value":"Huntsville occupied April 11, 1862","note":"Ormsby M. Mitchel’s division of the Army of the Ohio seized Huntsville and sections of the Memphis & Charleston Railroad while Buell moved toward Shiloh. The division remained exposed and depended on a long route from Nashville, local forage, and aggressive patrols."},{"label":"Kirby Smith’s position","value":"A thin Confederate line across eastern Tennessee","note":"Edmund Kirby Smith had to cover approaches from Cumberland Gap through Knoxville to Chattanooga with a force the episode estimates at fewer than 10,000."},{"label":"Cumberland Gap taken","value":"George W. Morgan occupied it June 18, 1862","note":"Morgan maneuvered through mountain passes to threaten the Confederate position. Carter L. Stevenson withdrew his garrison, allowing Morgan’s 7th Division to enter without a major battle."},{"label":"What the Gap offered","value":"A route toward Knoxville and the Virginia–Tennessee railroad","note":"The mountain pass could protect Kentucky, support Unionists in East Tennessee, and serve as a staging point for an advance against Confederate rail communications."},{"label":"Why Chattanooga mattered","value":"River crossing, railroad junction, mountain gateway, and supply hub","note":"Lines through Chattanooga connected Virginia with the western Confederacy and opened a route toward Atlanta and Georgia. Its terrain also made direct operations difficult."},{"label":"Next operational move","value":"Halleck directed Buell east from Corinth toward Chattanooga","note":"The episode ends in mid-June with the intended seizure of Chattanooga. The slow advance and Confederate response belong to the next phase of the western story."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“The New York Times Disunion: A History of the Civil War,” edited by Ted Widmer","note":"The volume collects selections from the New York Times Disunion series, which ran during the Civil War sesquicentennial."}],"maps":[{"title":"The western theater’s strategic network in mid-1862","caption":"This custom schematic connects the episode’s widely separated operations. Blue marks United States control or bases, red marks Confederate positions, and the route lines show Curtis’s march to Helena, Beauregard’s withdrawal to Tupelo, and the proposed United States axis toward Chattanooga. It is intentionally not to scale.","source":"Created for this guide from the episode transcript, U.S. Army campaign histories, National Park Service battle studies, and Library of Congress railroad and campaign maps; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep205-western-theater-network.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Missouri, Arkansas, Kentucky, Tennessee, Mississippi, Alabama, and Georgia showing rivers, railroads, strategic cities, Curtis’s route to Helena, Beauregard’s retreat to Tupelo, and the proposed Union advance to Chattanooga."},{"title":"Field fortifications and approaches at Corinth","caption":"Primary-source map: Army engineers recorded the United States approaches in blue and Confederate works in red. North is at the bottom, an unusual orientation that must be kept in mind when reading the map.","source":"Library of Congress Geography and Map Division; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep205-corinth-field-fortifications.jpg","alt":"Detailed 1862 manuscript map of Corinth showing roads, railroads, Union approaches, and Confederate fortifications in red and blue."},{"title":"Cumberland Gap from the south","caption":"Contextual image: this lithograph was published in 1862 from a drawing by United States Army topographical engineer Edward Ruger. It conveys the mountain barrier and fortified pass but is an interpreted print, not a photograph of Morgan’s June occupation.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep205-cumberland-gap-view.jpg","alt":"An 1862 lithograph looking north through Cumberland Gap, with mountain ridges, roads, camps, and fortifications labeled."},{"title":"The naval battle at Memphis","caption":"Contextual image: W. H. Morse’s circa-1887 engraving, after a drawing by Rear Adm. Henry Walke, looks south as United States gunboats and rams meet the Confederate River Defense Fleet. It was produced about twenty-five years after the battle.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep205-battle-of-memphis.jpg","alt":"Later nineteenth-century engraving of Union gunboats and rams engaging the Confederate fleet before Memphis on June 6, 1862."}],"moments":[{"time":"1:11","audioTime":71,"title":"The podcast returns to the western theater","detail":"The hosts resume the western story after nearly two years of episodes centered on campaigns in Virginia and Maryland."},{"time":"2:21","audioTime":141,"title":"The position after the spring campaigns","detail":"Confederate forces have abandoned Kentucky, much of Tennessee, and the railroad center at Corinth."},{"time":"2:32","audioTime":152,"title":"Three counteroffensives are introduced","detail":"The episode previews Confederate campaigns in Kentucky and Mississippi and the United States campaign that will culminate at Stones River."},{"time":"2:43","audioTime":163,"title":"The Upper South becomes the strategic prize","detail":"Kentucky and Tennessee matter for their population, agriculture, transport network, and position between northern territory and the Deep South."},{"time":"3:18","audioTime":198,"title":"Late 1862 is previewed as a net Confederate failure","detail":"The coming movements appear promising, but the episode’s overview anticipates that Confederate armies will again end on the defensive."},{"time":"4:40","audioTime":280,"title":"The spring 1862 disaster is recapped","detail":"Forts Henry and Donelson begin a chain of United States gains across the western theater."},{"time":"4:52","audioTime":292,"title":"Shiloh tested the advance","detail":"The Confederates tried to destroy Grant before Buell’s full arrival and halt the movement south."},{"time":"4:58","audioTime":298,"title":"Albert Sidney Johnston falls","detail":"Johnston is mortally wounded during the first day at Shiloh while the United States position holds near Pittsburg Landing."},{"time":"5:13","audioTime":313,"title":"Buell reinforces Grant","detail":"Fresh Army of the Ohio troops arrive overnight and join the April 7 counterattack."},{"time":"5:34","audioTime":334,"title":"Halleck takes personal field command","detail":"The department commander comes from St. Louis and combines the armies moving against Corinth."},{"time":"5:50","audioTime":350,"title":"Pope joins the concentration","detail":"The Army of the Mississippi adds its strength after the victory at Island No. 10."},{"time":"6:00","audioTime":360,"title":"The cautious advance begins","detail":"Halleck moves slowly from Pittsburg Landing and entrenches at each stage to prevent surprise."},{"time":"6:28","audioTime":388,"title":"Corinth produces siege work, not a climactic battle","detail":"Skirmishing accompanies the methodical approach, but Beauregard does not risk a major assault against superior numbers."},{"time":"6:36","audioTime":396,"title":"Beauregard evacuates Corinth","detail":"The Confederate army slips away during the night of May 29–30 and retreats toward Tupelo."},{"time":"6:56","audioTime":416,"title":"A major territorial gain is assessed","detail":"Taking Corinth completes an extraordinary first-half expansion of United States control in the west."},{"time":"7:03","audioTime":423,"title":"Kentucky and Tennessee shift","detail":"Kentucky is cleared of organized Confederate forces while much of western and central Tennessee is occupied or exposed."},{"time":"7:13","audioTime":433,"title":"Nashville’s fall is emphasized","detail":"The first Confederate state capital taken becomes a critical United States base."},{"time":"7:20","audioTime":440,"title":"The river defenses collapse north of Memphis","detail":"Island No. 10 and Fort Pillow no longer block United States movement on the upper Mississippi."},{"time":"7:35","audioTime":455,"title":"Memphis falls after a naval battle","detail":"United States gunboats and rams defeat the Confederate river fleet on June 6 and the city is occupied."},{"time":"7:51","audioTime":471,"title":"The survey moves west of the Mississippi","detail":"Samuel Curtis’s advance into Arkansas and victory at Pea Ridge widen the strategic view."},{"time":"8:08","audioTime":488,"title":"The New Mexico invasion fails","detail":"Logistical collapse and United States resistance end the Confederate attempt to carry the war north through New Mexico Territory."},{"time":"9:33","audioTime":573,"title":"Success creates a consolidation problem","detail":"The episode pivots from victories to the difficulty of holding a wide western theater and sustaining further advances."},{"time":"9:59","audioTime":599,"title":"Halleck disperses the Corinth concentration","detail":"Troops are assigned to key towns and supply routes rather than kept together for an immediate plunge south."},{"time":"10:14","audioTime":614,"title":"Logistics becomes the episode’s central subject","detail":"Rivers, railroads, roads, depots, and the distance from northern supply sources determine what the armies can do."},{"time":"10:40","audioTime":640,"title":"The southbound railroad bottleneck appears","detail":"Beyond the Corinth junction, a very large United States army would depend heavily on the Mobile & Ohio route into central Mississippi."},{"time":"10:57","audioTime":657,"title":"The army reaches the end of its immediate reach","detail":"The episode describes the captured network as the practical limit of the spring advance."},{"time":"11:01","audioTime":661,"title":"The Upper South’s advantages are listed","detail":"Established settlement, roads, railroads, nearby northern territory, and navigable rivers help sustain occupation."},{"time":"11:24","audioTime":684,"title":"Three rivers carried the advance","detail":"The Tennessee, Cumberland, and Mississippi offered deep transportation corridors for troops and supplies."},{"time":"11:36","audioTime":696,"title":"The Deep South presents a different problem","detail":"Greater distances and weaker inland transport make broad occupation and supply much harder."},{"time":"11:55","audioTime":715,"title":"Settlement and terrain shape supply","detail":"Plantations, pine forests, scattered farms, and long distances separate the Deep South’s limited urban centers."},{"time":"12:14","audioTime":734,"title":"Railroad coverage thins","detail":"Few cities and large areas without rail lines reduce the routes available to an advancing army."},{"time":"12:24","audioTime":744,"title":"Interior rivers offer little replacement","detail":"Many Alabama and Mississippi rivers are shallow or useful only farther south, leaving the Mississippi as the principal deep penetration."},{"time":"12:44","audioTime":764,"title":"The landlocked interior resists mass movement","detail":"Feeding and maneuvering large armies away from navigable water and rail becomes a defining constraint."},{"time":"13:02","audioTime":782,"title":"Occupation strategy reaches its limit","detail":"The dispersed garrison model used in the Upper South cannot simply be extended across the entire Deep South."},{"time":"13:37","audioTime":817,"title":"Geography dictates the next strategy","detail":"Commanders must select important objectives and prepare narrow campaigns capable of being supplied."},{"time":"13:52","audioTime":832,"title":"Two future corridors are identified","detail":"The Mississippi River and the railroad route from Chattanooga toward Atlanta illustrate the restricted axes available."},{"time":"14:21","audioTime":861,"title":"Movement becomes a measure of success","detail":"In the vast western theater, shifting and supplying armies across the map matters as much as battlefield combat."},{"time":"15:51","audioTime":951,"title":"The Corinth pause is reconsidered","detail":"After the advertisement break, the hosts argue that Halleck had legitimate supply reasons not to rush south."},{"time":"15:58","audioTime":958,"title":"Beauregard waits at Tupelo","detail":"The Confederate army is about sixty miles away, but pursuit would extend the United States army along a narrow supply route."},{"time":"16:07","audioTime":967,"title":"The cost of one vulnerable rail line","detail":"Food, ammunition, reinforcements, and evacuation of sick and wounded would all compete for the same corridor."},{"time":"16:55","audioTime":1015,"title":"Halleck looks west and east","detail":"He gives priority to consolidating gains and supporting exposed commands rather than pursuing directly south."},{"time":"17:11","audioTime":1031,"title":"Curtis is isolated in Arkansas","detail":"The Army of the Southwest is deep in hostile territory after Pea Ridge while Van Dorn has moved east to Corinth."},{"time":"17:32","audioTime":1052,"title":"The Rolla supply line stretches beyond 200 miles","detail":"Wagon trains must cross the Ozarks to support Curtis at Batesville."},{"time":"17:45","audioTime":1065,"title":"Little Rock is abandoned as an immediate objective","detail":"Curtis cuts loose from the failing overland line and turns southeast for Helena and river supply."},{"time":"18:00","audioTime":1080,"title":"A White River supply effort narrowly misses","detail":"Halleck’s relief fleet does not make the intended junction with Curtis’s moving army."},{"time":"18:05","audioTime":1085,"title":"Curtis reaches Helena","detail":"After living off the countryside, the Army of the Southwest arrives on July 12 and regains a dependable river connection."},{"time":"18:24","audioTime":1104,"title":"The strategic view turns east","detail":"Holding Tennessee proves more complicated than relieving Curtis because both sides had stripped broad areas to concentrate at Shiloh and Corinth."},{"time":"18:48","audioTime":1128,"title":"Large parts of Tennessee lack firm control","detail":"Outside Nashville and scattered posts, isolated detachments and contested countryside remain."},{"time":"19:05","audioTime":1145,"title":"Mitchel’s exposed division is introduced","detail":"About 8,000 Army of the Ohio troops have penetrated northern Alabama while Buell’s main body operated farther west."},{"time":"19:20","audioTime":1160,"title":"Huntsville sits on the east-west railroad","detail":"Mitchel occupies a point on the Memphis & Charleston line connecting Corinth and Chattanooga."},{"time":"19:37","audioTime":1177,"title":"Mitchel lives hand to mouth","detail":"His command relies on wagon supply from Nashville, local forage, and aggressive patrols against guerrillas."},{"time":"20:09","audioTime":1209,"title":"Kirby Smith is also overextended","detail":"The largest nearby Confederate command is dispersed across eastern Tennessee from Cumberland Gap to Chattanooga."},{"time":"20:32","audioTime":1232,"title":"Morgan takes Cumberland Gap","detail":"A United States division maneuvers against the pass in June and enters after the Confederate garrison withdraws."},{"time":"20:43","audioTime":1243,"title":"The Gap opens an approach to Knoxville","detail":"Possession threatens eastern Tennessee and the railroad connection toward Virginia."},{"time":"20:59","audioTime":1259,"title":"Chattanooga is endangered","detail":"The combined pressure from northern Alabama and Cumberland Gap makes Kirby Smith’s eastern Tennessee position precarious."},{"time":"21:09","audioTime":1269,"title":"Chattanooga is the gateway to Georgia","detail":"Its railroads and Tennessee River position could sustain a future United States invasion of the southeastern Confederacy."},{"time":"21:23","audioTime":1283,"title":"The east-west Confederate link runs through the city","detail":"Rail connections through Chattanooga help tie Virginia to the western Confederacy."},{"time":"21:32","audioTime":1292,"title":"Terrain reinforces Chattanooga’s value","detail":"The river, railroad junction, and surrounding mountains make the city a true strategic point."},{"time":"21:42","audioTime":1302,"title":"Halleck prepares a Chattanooga advance","detail":"By mid-June, the next intended move is east from Corinth and then into Georgia."},{"time":"21:54","audioTime":1314,"title":"Buell’s assignment sets up the next episode","detail":"The Army of the Ohio is directed toward Chattanooga, carrying the western story into its next phase."},{"time":"22:12","audioTime":1332,"title":"The book recommendation begins","detail":"The hosts recommend “The New York Times Disunion: A History of the Civil War,” edited by Ted Widmer."}],"command":[{"side":"United States civilian authority","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"President Abraham Lincoln","command":"President and commander in chief","subordinates":"Set national war policy and appointed senior Army and Navy leadership"},{"commander":"Secretary of War Edwin M. Stanton","command":"War Department","subordinates":"Civilian department responsible for the armies and the Army-operated river forces in this period"}]},{"side":"United States command at Corinth","color":"#47704f","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Henry W. Halleck","command":"Department of the Mississippi; personal commander of the Corinth concentration","subordinates":"Directed Grant, Buell, and Pope; became general in chief on July 11, 1862"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Ulysses S. Grant","command":"Army of the Tennessee","subordinates":"Halleck’s second in command during the Corinth advance, with reduced operational independence"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Don Carlos Buell","command":"Army of the Ohio","subordinates":"Brought reinforcements to Shiloh and was later assigned the advance from Corinth toward Chattanooga"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. John Pope","command":"Army of the Mississippi","subordinates":"Joined Halleck after Island No. 10 and led the left wing during the approach to Corinth"}]},{"side":"Other United States western commands","color":"#597aa5","branches":[{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Samuel R. Curtis","command":"Army of the Southwest","subordinates":"Defeated Van Dorn at Pea Ridge and moved by Batesville to Helena after abandoning the Rolla supply line"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Ormsby M. Mitchel","command":"3rd Division, Army of the Ohio","subordinates":"Occupied Huntsville and patrolled northern Alabama along the Memphis & Charleston Railroad"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. George W. Morgan","command":"7th Division, Army of the Ohio","subordinates":"Maneuvered against and occupied Cumberland Gap in June 1862"},{"commander":"Flag Officer Charles H. Davis and Col. Charles Ellet","command":"Western Gunboat Flotilla and United States Ram Fleet","subordinates":"Led the cooperating river forces that defeated the Confederate fleet at Memphis"}]},{"side":"Confederate western command","color":"#963b31","branches":[{"commander":"President Jefferson Davis","command":"Confederate president and commander in chief","subordinates":"Set national strategy and appointed the senior commanders responsible for the western departments"},{"commander":"Gen. Albert Sidney Johnston","command":"Western Department until mortally wounded at Shiloh","subordinates":"Concentrated the Confederate army at Corinth and attacked Grant on April 6"},{"commander":"Gen. P. G. T. Beauregard","command":"Army of the Mississippi after Johnston’s death","subordinates":"Retreated from Shiloh, defended Corinth, and evacuated the army to Tupelo"},{"commander":"Gen. Braxton Bragg","command":"Army of the Mississippi from June 1862","subordinates":"Succeeded Beauregard and prepared the Confederate return to the strategic offensive"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Earl Van Dorn","command":"Army of the West","subordinates":"Commanded at Pea Ridge, then transferred his army east to reinforce the Corinth concentration"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Edmund Kirby Smith","command":"Department of East Tennessee","subordinates":"Held the line from Cumberland Gap through Knoxville to Chattanooga; Carter L. Stevenson commanded the Gap garrison"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Henry W. Halleck","role":"United States department commander who combined three armies at Corinth and then dispersed them to secure the western logistical network","side":"United States","source":"https://history.army.mil/Research/Reference-Topics/Army-Campaigns/Brief-Summaries/Civil-War/"},{"personId":"ulysses-s-grant","role":"Commander of the Army of the Tennessee at Shiloh and during Halleck’s advance on Corinth"},{"name":"Don Carlos Buell","role":"Army of the Ohio commander whose troops reinforced Shiloh and who received the assignment to advance toward Chattanooga","side":"United States","source":"https://www.nps.gov/cane/highest-praise-the-army-of-the-ohio-at-shiloh.htm"},{"name":"John Pope","role":"Army of the Mississippi commander who joined Halleck after the capture of Island No. 10","side":"United States","source":"https://history.army.mil/Research/Reference-Topics/Army-Campaigns/Brief-Summaries/Civil-War/"},{"name":"Samuel R. Curtis","role":"Army of the Southwest commander at Pea Ridge who cut loose from his supply line and marched to Helena","side":"United States","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/learn/historyculture/curtis.htm"},{"name":"Ormsby M. Mitchel","role":"Army of the Ohio division commander who occupied Huntsville and operated along the Memphis & Charleston Railroad","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep205-ormsby-mitchel-portrait.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2003656379/"},{"name":"George W. Morgan","role":"United States division commander who maneuvered against and occupied Cumberland Gap in June 1862","side":"United States","source":"https://www.nps.gov/cuga/learn/news/blue-and-gray-in-black-and-white.htm"},{"name":"Charles H. Davis","role":"Flag officer commanding the Western Gunboat Flotilla at the Battle of Memphis","side":"United States","source":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=tn004"},{"name":"Charles Ellet","role":"Colonel commanding the United States Ram Fleet at Memphis, where he received a mortal wound","side":"United States","source":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=tn004"},{"name":"Albert Sidney Johnston","role":"Confederate western commander mortally wounded while leading the attack at Shiloh","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=tn003"},{"name":"P. G. T. Beauregard","role":"Johnston’s successor who withdrew from Shiloh, defended Corinth, and evacuated to Tupelo","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/the-siege-and-battle-of-corinth-a-new-kind-of-war-teaching-with-historic-places.htm"},{"name":"Braxton Bragg","role":"Beauregard’s successor whose coming Kentucky campaign is one of the three Confederate counteroffensives previewed","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-7.pdf"},{"name":"Earl Van Dorn","role":"Confederate commander defeated at Pea Ridge who transferred east and later attempted to retake Corinth","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/the-pea-ridge-campaign.htm"},{"name":"Sterling Price","role":"Missouri State Guard commander who joined Van Dorn’s Confederate concentration for Pea Ridge","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/the-pea-ridge-campaign.htm"},{"name":"Edmund Kirby Smith","role":"Confederate department commander responsible for eastern Tennessee from Cumberland Gap to Chattanooga","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.nps.gov/cuga/learn/news/blue-and-gray-in-black-and-white.htm"},{"name":"Carter L. Stevenson","role":"Confederate commander who withdrew the Cumberland Gap garrison before Morgan’s June occupation","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.nps.gov/cuga/learn/news/blue-and-gray-in-black-and-white.htm"},{"name":"Henry H. Sibley","role":"Confederate commander whose New Mexico invasion collapsed after the loss of his supply train at Glorieta Pass","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.nps.gov/foun/learn/historyculture/the-star-fort.htm"}],"losses":[{"name":"Gen. Albert Sidney Johnston","side":"Confederacy","role":"Commander of the Confederate Western Department and army at Shiloh","outcome":"Mortally wounded on April 6, 1862, during the first day at Shiloh; died on the battlefield"},{"name":"Col. Charles Ellet","side":"United States","role":"Commander of the United States Ram Fleet at Memphis","outcome":"Mortally wounded during the Battle of Memphis on June 6, 1862; died on June 21"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep205-western-theater-network.svg","alt":"Schematic western theater map showing control, river and rail corridors, and the principal movements discussed in episode 205.","caption":"The episode’s story is a network problem: armies could move only as far as their rivers, railroads, roads, depots, and forage could sustain them.","source":"Custom guide graphic based on U.S. Army, National Park Service, and Library of Congress sources."},{"image":"/images/ep205-corinth-field-fortifications.jpg","alt":"Manuscript military map of Union approaches and Confederate defenses around Corinth in May 1862.","caption":"Primary-source map: the colored approach and defense lines show why Corinth was a siege of fieldworks and careful movement rather than a single set-piece assault.","source":"Library of Congress Geography and Map Division."},{"image":"/images/ep205-ormsby-mitchel-portrait.jpg","alt":"Nineteenth-century full-length lithographic portrait of United States Brigadier General Ormsby M. Mitchel in uniform.","caption":"Contextual image: this Currier & Ives portrait identifies the division commander whose exposed northern Alabama operations anchor the episode’s eastern flank.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division."},{"image":"/images/ep205-cumberland-gap-view.jpg","alt":"An 1862 lithographic view looking north through Cumberland Gap.","caption":"Contextual image: the contemporary lithograph is an interpreted landscape, not a photograph, but it makes the pass’s restrictive terrain immediately legible.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division."},{"image":"/images/ep205-battle-of-memphis.jpg","alt":"Circa-1887 engraving of the Battle of Memphis with United States warships in the foreground and the Confederate fleet approaching.","caption":"Contextual image: the later engraving depicts the June 6 naval action that opened Memphis and another long section of the Mississippi River to United States control.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 205 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/category/1862/page/5/"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Civil War in the Western Theater, 1862","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-7.pdf"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: Civil War campaign summaries","url":"https://history.army.mil/Research/Reference-Topics/Army-Campaigns/Brief-Summaries/Civil-War/"},{"label":"National Park Service: Corinth Battlefield Unit","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/planyourvisit/corinth.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: The Siege and Battle of Corinth","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/the-siege-and-battle-of-corinth-a-new-kind-of-war-teaching-with-historic-places.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service battle detail: Corinth I","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=ms016"},{"label":"Primary-source map: field fortifications around Corinth","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99446429/"},{"label":"National Park Service battle detail: Memphis I","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=tn004"},{"label":"Library of Congress: The Battle of Memphis, looking south","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004667779/"},{"label":"National Park Service: The Pea Ridge Campaign","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/the-pea-ridge-campaign.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: The Outcome of the Pea Ridge Campaign","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/the-outcome.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Civil War in the Trans-Mississippi Theater, 1861–1865","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-3.pdf"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of Glorieta Pass","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peco/learn/historyculture/battle-of-glorieta-pass.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Army of the Ohio at Shiloh","url":"https://www.nps.gov/cane/highest-praise-the-army-of-the-ohio-at-shiloh.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Alabama Civil War operations list","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/alabama.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Civil War at Cumberland Gap","url":"https://www.nps.gov/cuga/civil-war-at-cumberland-gap.htm"},{"label":"Primary-source-derived map: George W. Morgan’s 1862 Cumberland Gap operations","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/lva0000034/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: View of Cumberland Gap from the south","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2013645292/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: O. M. Mitchel portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2003656379/"},{"label":"Primary source: Official Records, western theater, June–October 1862","url":"https://www.loc.gov/resource/llserialsetce.02419_00_00-002-0273-0000/"}]}}]}]
12:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section maps-section","id":"maps","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Maps"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"maps-grid","children":[["$","figure","Field fortifications and approaches at Corinth",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep205-corinth-field-fortifications.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open Field fortifications and approaches at Corinth at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep205-corinth-field-fortifications.jpg","alt":"Detailed 1862 manuscript map of Corinth showing roads, railroads, Union approaches, and Confederate fortifications in red and blue.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Field fortifications and approaches at Corinth"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Primary-source map: Army engineers recorded the United States approaches in blue and Confederate works in red. North is at the bottom, an unusual orientation that must be kept in mind when reading the map."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep205-corinth-field-fortifications.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Library of Congress Geography and Map Division; see sources below."}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}],["$","figure","Cumberland Gap from the south",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep205-cumberland-gap-view.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open Cumberland Gap from the south at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep205-cumberland-gap-view.jpg","alt":"An 1862 lithograph looking north through Cumberland Gap, with mountain ridges, roads, camps, and fortifications labeled.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Cumberland Gap from the south"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Contextual image: this lithograph was published in 1862 from a drawing by United States Army topographical engineer Edward Ruger. It conveys the mountain barrier and fortified pass but is an interpreted print, not a photograph of Morgan’s June occupation."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep205-cumberland-gap-view.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}],["$","figure","The naval battle at Memphis",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep205-battle-of-memphis.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open The naval battle at Memphis at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep205-battle-of-memphis.jpg","alt":"Later nineteenth-century engraving of Union gunboats and rams engaging the Confederate fleet before Memphis on June 6, 1862.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"The naval battle at Memphis"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Contextual image: W. H. Morse’s circa-1887 engraving, after a drawing by Rear Adm. Henry Walke, looks south as United States gunboats and rams meet the Confederate River Defense Fleet. It was produced about twenty-five years after the battle."}],"$L24","$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]
